 "client": {
  "headline": "When gold jumps and stocks are already soft, what has the S&P done next?",
  "summary": "Looking at 18 such episodes since 2000, the S&P 500 was higher a month later about two times in three, with a typical gain of about 3%. That is better than an ordinary month, but the spread of outcomes is wide. The pattern has been strongest in a Goldilocks economy and softest in stagflation. We would treat it as a reason to lean in with a defined-risk structure, not as a standalone call."
